  <h2 id="claim-gap">How to claim and check your child’s account</h2>
  <p>Check your child’s account status and balance through the authorized service linked from <a href={portal}>TrumpAccounts.gov</a>. Child Savings Lab cannot look up your child’s account or balance.</p>
  <p>TrumpAccounts.gov is the official government portal. For a safer sign-in, use its links to the app or account service. Treasury and its authorized providers say they will never ask for a password or a one-time verification code by email, text, or phone. The <a href={irsAccounts}>IRS also links to the authorized account provider</a>.</p>
  <ClaimSnapshot />
  <p>On October 29, 2026, the <a href={irsDepositSnapshot}>IRS reported nearly $9 billion in contributions across nearly 70 million accounts</a>. Dividing those rounded totals gives a rough program-wide average of $129 per account. On November 24, <a href={programDeposits}>TrumpAccounts.gov reported that total deposits had exceeded $9 billion</a>. Check the official account service for your child’s own balance.</p>
  <ClaimBenefits />
  <p>At the <a href={stockReporting}>November 25 close</a>, two shares for all 70 million accounts would be worth about $17.8 billion. That is an illustration assuming every account qualifies. The <a href={treasuryAnnualReport}>Treasury totals</a> suggest roughly 10 million accounts outside the initial auto-enrolled group. How many of those earlier accounts are active and whether they qualify for the shares are unconfirmed.</p>
  <p><a href={spacexAnnouncement}>SpaceX’s November 25 release</a> also reports a separate personal pledge of more than 2 million shares from its president, Gwynne Shotwell. Distribution details are unknown.</p>
  <h3>Two examples with different birth years</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A U.S. citizen born in 2025 who meets the other IRS requirements and has an approved pilot election could receive the <a href={irsAccounts}>$1,000 federal contribution</a>.</li>
    <li>A child born in 2020 in a qualifying ZIP code could have received the <a href={dellGift}>$250 Dell contribution</a>. Check the account’s transaction history.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The two programs cover different birth years. Confirm stock eligibility and any delivery separately.</p>
  <p><a href="/sources/#claim-update-sources">Read the dated claim and gift sources</a>.</p>
  <TrumpRuleNote />
  <p class="result-help"><strong>What changed November 27, 2026.</strong> Added claim guidance, dated claim figures, gift conditions and the newly announced share pledge so families can tell which details to check for their own account.</p>
  <h2 id="overview">What a Trump Account is</h2>
  <p>A Trump Account is a type of traditional individual retirement account owned by the beneficiary. Its “growth period” begins when the initial account is established and ends on December 31 of the calendar year in which the beneficiary turns 17.</p>
  <h2 id="auto-enrollment">Automatic enrollment</h2>
  <p>Treasury’s temporary regulations provide for an auto account for each person Treasury determines meets the age and Social Security number requirements and has no prior election. Periodic enrollment is expected to make elections by other people rare.</p>
  <p>The baseline rules generally require the individual to be under age 18 at the end of the election year and to have an SSN within the meaning of Internal Revenue Code section 24(h)(7)—generally an SSN valid for employment—issued before the election.</p>
  <p>Children without a qualifying Social Security number could not be automatically enrolled. If your child needs a first SSN, start with the <a href={ssaFirstNumber}>Social Security Administration’s application instructions</a> and use <a href={irsAccounts}>IRS eligibility guidance</a> to check the account requirements.</p>
  <h2 id="claim-and-activate">Claiming and activating an auto account</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Start with the app links at <a href={portal}>TrumpAccounts.gov</a> and check whether Treasury already opened an account.</li>
    <li>Follow the <a href={irsAccounts}>IRS claim instructions</a> to verify your identity and authority. Under <a href={temporaryRule}>TD 10056</a>, that usually means a guardian or custodian with authority under state law, or a beneficiary with legal capacity.</li>
    <li>Follow the <a href={programDeposits}>official claiming instructions</a> to review the child’s information and accept the account terms. Follow any remaining activation instructions from the receiving trustee.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A claim alone does not establish the receiving account. The receiving trustee must provide activation instructions, including an account agreement. Until the receiving account is established and the balance can be transferred, the assets remain in the auto account.</p>
  <h2 id="contribution-readiness">Why account state matters for contributions</h2>
  <p>During the growth period, an unclaimed auto account generally accepts only qualified general contributions and an eligible <a href="https://www.irs.gov/trumpaccounts">$1,000 pilot contribution</a>. Family, friend and employer contributions require the receiving account to be claimed and activated. The <a href={portal}>government portal</a> directs families to claim to receive and manage the pilot benefit, while <a href={temporaryRule}>TD 10056</a> permits Treasury to deposit an elected pilot contribution into an auto account. Check the official record for your deposit and next step.</p>
  <h2 id="pilot-election">The separate <a href="https://www.irs.gov/trumpaccounts">$1,000 pilot election</a></h2>
  <p>Treasury cannot make the pilot election automatically. In addition to the baseline account requirements, the child generally must be a U.S. citizen <a href="https://www.irs.gov/trumpaccounts">born from January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028</a>, and the person making the election must expect the child to be their qualifying child for that year. Check whether an election is pending or processed before submitting another one.</p>
  <p><a class="button" href="/tools/trump-account-eligibility-checker/">Check the account path and pilot status</a></p>
  <h2 id="contributions">Contributions and investments</h2>
  <p>For 2026 and 2027, most family, individual, and qualifying Section 128 employer contributions share a <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4547">$5,000 annual limit</a>. Section 128 employer contributions have a <a href="https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-52_IRB">$2,500 per-employee ceiling</a> within that total. The pilot amount, qualified general contributions, and qualified rollovers are excluded from the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i4547">$5,000 limit</a>.</p>
  <p>During the growth period, ordinary assets are limited to qualifying index mutual funds or ETFs. An eligible investment fund may not impose annual fees and expenses above 0.10%; separate trustee or intermediary fees are analyzed separately.</p>
  <h2 id="withdrawals">Withdrawals and the age-18 transition</h2>
  <p>Distributions are tightly limited during the growth period. Starting January 1 of the year the beneficiary turns 18, most traditional IRA rules generally apply. Taxable early distributions may face an additional 10% tax unless an exception applies.</p>
  <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Check the official account status before making another election.</li>
    <li>Use the <a href="/forms/form-4547-guide/">updated Form 4547 guide</a> for the pilot election or a rare manual exception.</li>
    <li>Model only activated-account contributions with the <a href="/tools/trump-account-growth-calculator/">calendar-aware growth calculator</a>.</li>
    <li>Confirm current procedures at the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/trumpaccounts">official IRS page</a>.</li>
  </ul>
